<Base title="About" description="How Kvali chooses treks, where its data comes from, and credits.">
  <h1>About Kvali</h1>

  <h2>Which treks are listed</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>At least 3 days of walking.</li>
    <li>A named, defined route.</li>
    <li>Doable on your own: no mandatory guide and no permit lottery. Free registrations are allowed and shown on the trek.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How the data is made</h2>
  <p>
    Each trek is researched from public sources and checked by hand before it is published. Facts that matter most to a
    walker (best months, where you sleep, permits, getting there) each link to their source on the trek page. Distance and
    ascent are computed from the route line, not copied from other sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Credits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Route lines: © <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap contributors</a>, under the Open
      Database License. The route files published on this site are offered under the same licence.
    </li>
    <li>Base map: <a href="https://openfreemap.org/">OpenFreeMap</a>, © <a href="https://openmaptiles.org/">OpenMapTiles</a>, data from OpenStreetMap.</li>
    <li>
      Terrain and elevation: <a href="https://registry.opendata.aws/terrain-tiles/">Terrain Tiles on AWS</a>, built from
      open elevation data. See their <a href="https://github.com/tilezen/joerd/blob/master/docs/attribution.md">list of sources</a>.
    </li>
    <li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons. Author and licence are shown under each photo.</li>
    <li>Trip reports belong to their authors. Kvali links to them and writes its own one-line summary.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
